// Class 6 Science practice bank, part 3: The Wonderful World of Science to Materials Around Us (Curiosity), new questions.
// Unreviewed: a teacher must check before sale.
StudyData.addPractice(6, "science", {
  "the-wonderful-world-of-science": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which branch of science studies the weather and the Earth itself?", options: ["Zoology", "Chemistry", "Earth science", "Botany"], answer: 2, explain: "Earth science studies the Earth and its weather. Zoology and botany study animals and plants, and chemistry studies substances.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Curiosity in science means", options: ["believing whatever others say", "wanting to find out how and why things happen", "memorising answers from a book", "avoiding questions that are new"], answer: 1, explain: "Science begins with curiosity: asking why and how. Accepting everything without asking, or only memorising, does not lead to discoveries.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Carefully noticing and recording what happens, using our senses and instruments, is called", options: ["hypothesis", "observation", "conclusion", "technology"], answer: 1, explain: "This is observation. A hypothesis is a testable guess and a conclusion is drawn at the end.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A planned activity done to test a hypothesis is called an", options: ["experiment", "observation", "conclusion", "question"], answer: 0, explain: "We test a hypothesis by doing an experiment. An observation only notes what we see.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these should never be done in a science activity?", options: ["Tasting an unknown chemical to find out what it is", "Washing hands after the activity", "Listening to the teacher's instructions first", "Using a cloth to hold a hot object"], answer: 0, explain: "Chemicals must never be tasted, because some are harmful. The other three are safe habits.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The study of plants is called", options: ["zoology", "botany", "astronomy", "physics"], answer: 1, explain: "Botany is the study of plants, zoology of animals and astronomy of space objects. All of botany and zoology belong to biology.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A pressure cooker, a bicycle and a mobile phone show that science is", options: ["found only in laboratories", "useful only to grown-ups", "limited to the pages of a book", "all around us, not only in laboratories"], answer: 3, explain: "Science is in our kitchens, roads and homes, not just in labs.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which quality means reporting exactly what you found, even if it is not what you expected?", options: ["Honesty", "Patience", "Curiosity", "Teamwork"], answer: 0, explain: "Honesty means recording and reporting what really happened. Patience is waiting calmly and curiosity is wanting to find out.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these questions can be investigated by an experiment?", options: ["Which flower in the garden is the most beautiful?", "Does a seed sprout faster in warm soil than in cold soil?", "Should the school day start at 8 or 9 o'clock?", "Is it kind to share your lunch?"], answer: 1, explain: "Only a question about something we can measure and compare can be tested. Beauty, rules and kindness are matters of opinion.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Learning that a curved piece of glass bends light is science. Making spectacles using this idea is", options: ["technology", "a hypothesis", "an observation", "a fair test"], answer: 0, explain: "Using scientific knowledge to make a useful thing is technology.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A ball takes the same time to roll down a slope in five repeated trials. This shows that", options: ["the hypothesis can never be changed", "the experiment was not needed", "the result is reliable", "the observations were recorded wrongly"], answer: 2, explain: "A result that comes again and again is reliable. Even then, a hypothesis may change if new evidence appears.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Rohan studies how an iron nail turns into a brown flaky substance when left in damp air. This is mainly a question for", options: ["astronomy", "botany", "chemistry", "earth science"], answer: 2, explain: "Chemistry studies how substances change into new substances. Rust is a new substance.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which pair is correctly matched?", options: ["Botany – study of animals", "Physics – study of living things", "Zoology – study of animals", "Chemistry – study of the Moon and stars"], answer: 2, explain: "Zoology studies animals. Botany studies plants, physics studies force, motion and energy, and astronomy studies the Moon and stars.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why do we change only one thing at a time in a fair test?", options: ["So that we know exactly which change caused the result", "So that the experiment finishes sooner", "So that less material is needed", "So that the result is always what we expected"], answer: 0, explain: "If two things are changed together, we cannot tell which one caused the result.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Rahul measures a plant's height every 3 days for 15 days. What is the best way to record this?", options: ["Writing 'the plant grew well' in the notebook", "Remembering the heights by heart", "Drawing the plant once at the end", "A table with the day and the height in cm"], answer: 3, explain: "A table with units lets us compare the readings easily and not forget them.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A student's experiment shows that her hypothesis was wrong. What is the best thing for her to do?", options: ["Change the recorded results to match the hypothesis", "Say that the experiment is useless and stop", "Say that science cannot answer such questions", "Accept the result and make a new hypothesis"], answer: 3, explain: "A wrong hypothesis is still useful, because it teaches us something and leads to a better guess.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Science keeps growing because", options: ["scientific ideas can change when new evidence is found", "scientists never agree on anything", "all old ideas are always wrong", "books keep getting longer"], answer: 0, explain: "New evidence can improve or correct earlier ideas. That is how science grows.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Fatima says, 'I think a plant gets taller if it gets more sunlight.' This statement is a", options: ["conclusion", "measurement", "fact already proved", "hypothesis"], answer: 3, explain: "It is a testable guess that can be checked by an experiment, so it is a hypothesis. A conclusion is drawn after testing.", level: 2 },
      { q: "After analysing the results of an experiment, what is the next step in the scientific method before sharing?", options: ["Making the first observation", "Asking the question", "Making the hypothesis", "Drawing a conclusion"], answer: 3, explain: "The order is observe, ask, hypothesise, experiment, analyse, conclude and then share.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which scientist is behaving most like a good scientist?", options: ["Dev throws away readings that do not fit his guess", "Kavya checks the thermometer again after a surprising reading and repeats the test", "Ria copies her friend's table of results", "Tarun stops after one trial because it matched his guess"], answer: 1, explain: "Good scientists are honest and careful, and they repeat tests. The others hide, copy or trust one trial.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Gurpreet's hypothesis was that heavier stones sink faster. Her results showed no difference, but she writes 'heavier stones sink faster' in her report. What is wrong with this?", options: ["Nothing is wrong, because a hypothesis must always be proved", "She should not have made a hypothesis", "She has not reported what she really found", "Stones cannot be tested in an experiment"], answer: 2, explain: "Scientists report their real results, even when they do not match the hypothesis. A hypothesis can be proved wrong.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Four pots have the same soil and seeds. Pot 1: sun, 50 mL water. Pot 2: shade, 50 mL water. Pot 3: sun, 100 mL water. Pot 4: shade, 100 mL water. Which two pots should be compared to test the effect of water alone, with sunlight the same?", options: ["Pot 1 and Pot 2", "Pot 1 and Pot 3", "Pot 1 and Pot 4", "Pot 2 and Pot 3"], answer: 1, explain: "Pots 1 and 3 differ only in the amount of water (both in sun). Pots 1 and 2 differ only in light. Pots 1 and 4 and pots 2 and 3 differ in both.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A new medicine is made using knowledge of how germs grow. Which statement describes this best?", options: ["Technology gives the knowledge and biology makes the product", "Science and technology mean exactly the same thing", "Biology gives the knowledge and technology uses it to make a useful product", "Only chemistry is involved in making it"], answer: 2, explain: "Science helps us understand nature and technology uses that knowledge to make useful things.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A student grows one pot of beans with music and one without, and the music pot is taller. She writes, 'Music makes plants grow better.' Why is this not yet a sound conclusion?", options: ["Music cannot be tested at all", "A conclusion must be written before the experiment", "Only teachers are allowed to draw conclusions", "One trial may be due to chance, so the test should be repeated and made fair"], answer: 3, explain: "A single trial is not enough. We must keep other things the same and repeat the test to be sure.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Sachin wants to test whether a steel bottle with a white cover keeps water cool longer than one with a black cover. Which of these must he keep the same?", options: ["Both bottles' size, the amount and starting temperature of water, and the place they are kept", "The colour of the cover on both bottles", "The time at which each bottle is opened", "The final temperature of the water in both bottles"], answer: 0, explain: "Only the colour of the cover is changed. The final temperature is the result and cannot be kept the same.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What does the word 'science' come from, and what does it mean?", a: "The word 'science' comes from the Latin word 'scientia', which means 'knowledge'.", marks: 1, type: "short" },
      { q: "Give two examples of science in an Indian kitchen.", a: "Any two: a pressure cooker cooks food faster because of steam; milk turns into curd; heating changes raw food into cooked food; a mixer grinder uses electricity.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Name any two branches of science and write what each one studies.", a: "Physics studies matter, energy, force, motion, light and heat. Chemistry studies substances and how they change into new substances. (Biology: living things; astronomy: stars and planets also accepted.)", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "What is technology? Give two examples.", a: "Technology is the use of scientific knowledge to make useful things. Examples: vaccines, satellites, mobile phones.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Why should a scientist never change a recorded result to match the hypothesis?", a: "Scientists must be honest. The real result may show that the hypothesis is wrong, and an unexpected result can lead to a better hypothesis.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "What is an observation? How is it different from a hypothesis?", a: "An observation is carefully noticing and recording what happens using our senses and instruments. A hypothesis is a testable guess that explains an observation.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Rohit tested once and says his conclusion is final. Do you agree? Give a reason.", a: "No. One result may be affected by a mistake or by chance. The experiment should be repeated, and if the same result comes again and again, we can trust the conclusion.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Priya notices that clothes dry faster on a sunny day. Describe how she can use the steps of the scientific method to find out why.", a: "Observation: clothes dry faster on a sunny day.\nQuestion: does sunlight make clothes dry faster?\nHypothesis: clothes dry faster in sunlight than in shade.\nExperiment (fair test): hang two identical wet cloths with the same amount of water, one in sunlight and one in shade, and compare the time taken to dry.\nAnalyse the results and draw a conclusion; repeat the test and share the findings.", marks: 4, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Harpreet's tomato plants near the tube well grew taller than those at the far end of his field. He thinks that the extra water is the reason. He decides to test it by giving extra water to some plants at the far end.\n(i) What was Harpreet's observation?\n(ii) Write his hypothesis.\n(iii) Name one thing that he must keep the same to make it a fair test.", a: "(i) Plants near the tube well grew taller than those at the far end.\n(ii) Plants that get more water grow taller.\n(iii) Any one: the type of tomato plant, the soil, the size of the plants, the sunlight (only the amount of water is changed).", marks: 4, type: "case" },
      { q: "A company says that its new tablet makes a plant grow twice as tall. Rahul tests it by putting the tablet in a plant kept on a sunny balcony and compares it with another plant in a dark room. (a) Why is his test not fair? (b) Suggest how to correct it. (c) Why should he repeat the test?", a: "(a) The two plants differ in light as well as in the tablet, so we cannot tell which caused the difference.\n(b) Take two similar plants of the same kind, size and soil; keep them in the same place with the same water and light; give the tablet to only one.\n(c) Repeating shows whether the result is reliable and not due to chance or a mistake.", marks: 5, type: "hots" }
    ]
  },
  "diversity-in-the-living-world": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of these plants has a thick, hard, woody trunk?", options: ["Mint", "Neem", "Coriander", "Wheat"], answer: 1, explain: "Neem is a tree, tall with a woody trunk. Mint, coriander and wheat are herbs with soft stems.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A rose plant is bushy, with many hard but thin branches growing from near the ground. It is a", options: ["shrub", "herb", "tree", "creeper"], answer: 0, explain: "A bushy plant with hard thin stems from near the base is a shrub. A tree has a thick trunk and a herb has a soft stem.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Tiny living things such as bacteria are called", options: ["microorganisms", "herbs", "shrubs", "habitats"], answer: 0, explain: "Living things are studied as plants, animals and microorganisms, which are too small to see easily.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The stalk that joins a leaf to the stem is called the", options: ["lamina", "vein", "petiole", "trunk"], answer: 2, explain: "The petiole is the leaf stalk. The lamina is the flat blade and veins are the lines in it.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Pea and grapevine plants climb up a support using", options: ["spines", "tendrils", "a thick trunk", "fibrous roots"], answer: 1, explain: "Climbers have weak stems and hold on to a support with tendrils.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is a terrestrial habitat?", options: ["A pond", "A desert", "A river", "The sea"], answer: 1, explain: "Terrestrial habitats are on land, such as forests, deserts, grasslands and mountains. Ponds, rivers and the sea are aquatic.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A root system with one main thick root and thin side roots is called a", options: ["fibrous root", "parallel root", "reticulate root", "taproot"], answer: 3, explain: "This is a taproot, as in carrot or mustard. Reticulate and parallel are kinds of leaf veins, not roots.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A leaf whose veins form a net-like pattern is said to have", options: ["reticulate venation", "parallel venation", "fibrous venation", "taproot venation"], answer: 0, explain: "Net-like veins are reticulate venation, as in mango and peepal. Veins running side by side are parallel venation.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which habitat and animal are correctly paired?", options: ["Thar Desert – Bengal tiger", "Kaziranga grasslands – one-horned rhinoceros", "Sundarbans mangroves – one-horned rhinoceros", "Kaziranga grasslands – camel"], answer: 1, explain: "The Bengal tiger lives in the Sundarbans mangroves, the one-horned rhinoceros in Kaziranga and the camel in desert areas.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why are the broad padded feet of a camel useful in a desert?", options: ["They stop the feet from sinking into loose sand", "They store water for many days", "They help it swim in rivers", "They keep its hump cool"], answer: 0, explain: "Broad padded feet spread the weight on soft sand. The hump stores fat, not water.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The hump of a camel stores", options: ["water", "sand", "fat", "air"], answer: 2, explain: "The hump is a store of fat which the camel can use when food is scarce. It is a common mistake to think it stores water.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why do we group living things?", options: ["Because all living things are exactly alike", "So that they can all live in the same place", "To study the huge variety of life more easily by putting similar ones together", "Because only grouped things can survive"], answer: 2, explain: "Grouping by features is like arranging items in a shop, and it makes studying easier.", level: 2 },
      { q: "How can you tell a climber from a creeper in a field?", options: ["A climber goes up on a support using tendrils, while a creeper spreads along the ground", "A climber spreads along the ground, while a creeper goes up on a support", "A climber has a woody trunk, while a creeper has none", "A climber has a fibrous root, while a creeper has a taproot"], answer: 0, explain: "Both have weak stems. What differs is whether the plant climbs a support or spreads on the ground.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of these is a function of the stem?", options: ["To make food using sunlight", "To absorb water from the soil", "To hold the plant firmly in the soil", "To carry water and minerals up to the leaves"], answer: 3, explain: "Making food is done by leaves, and absorbing water and holding the plant in the soil are jobs of the roots.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Leaves make food for the plant using", options: ["sunlight, soil and oxygen", "only water and minerals from the roots", "sunlight, water and carbon dioxide", "carbon dioxide and soil"], answer: 2, explain: "A leaf needs sunlight, water and carbon dioxide to make food.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which one is the odd one out: wheat, maize, mustard, grass?", options: ["Wheat", "Mustard", "Maize", "Grass"], answer: 1, explain: "Mustard has a taproot and net-like veins. Wheat, maize and grass have fibrous roots and parallel veins.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A herb is different from a tree because a herb has", options: ["a thick woody trunk", "a weak stem that climbs", "a soft, green, tender stem", "many hard branches from near the base"], answer: 2, explain: "Herbs such as mint and coriander have soft stems. Trees have a woody trunk and shrubs have many hard thin branches near the base.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which feature best helps an animal to walk on loose desert sand?", options: ["Gills and fins", "A streamlined body", "Thin legs with sharp hooves", "Long legs and broad padded feet"], answer: 3, explain: "Long legs and broad padded feet, as in a camel, stop it from sinking. Gills and fins are for living in water.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which step helps to protect biodiversity?", options: ["Cutting forests to make more farmland", "Draining ponds to build houses", "Catching as many wild animals as possible", "Setting up national parks and wildlife sanctuaries"], answer: 3, explain: "National parks and sanctuaries protect habitats. The other actions destroy habitats.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A student sorts plants into Group 1 (tall, thick woody trunk), Group 2 (bushy, many hard thin stems) and Group 3 (small, soft stem). Where do neem, hibiscus and coriander go?", options: ["Neem in Group 1, hibiscus in Group 2, coriander in Group 3", "Neem in Group 2, hibiscus in Group 1, coriander in Group 3", "Neem in Group 3, hibiscus in Group 2, coriander in Group 1", "Neem in Group 1, hibiscus in Group 3, coriander in Group 2"], answer: 0, explain: "Neem is a tree, hibiscus is a shrub and coriander is a herb.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A plant is about 10 m tall with a thick woody trunk, a taproot and leaves with net-like veins. Which plant is it most likely to be?", options: ["Wheat", "Coriander", "Pumpkin", "Peepal"], answer: 3, explain: "A tall woody plant is a tree and peepal is a tree with a taproot and net-like veins. Wheat and coriander are herbs and pumpkin is a creeper.", level: 3 },
      { q: "If the spines of a cactus were replaced by large flat leaves, what would most likely happen in the desert?", options: ["It would store more water", "It would grow a taproot overnight", "Nothing would change", "It would lose more water and might dry out"], answer: 3, explain: "The spines are leaves reduced in size to cut water loss. Large flat leaves would lose much more water.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Plant A: soft green stem, fibrous roots, parallel veins. Plant B: thick woody trunk, taproot, net-like veins. Plant C: weak stem spreading along the ground, taproot, net-like veins. Plant D: many hard thin stems from near the base, taproot, net-like veins. Which row names them correctly?", options: ["A – shrub, B – tree, C – climber, D – herb", "A – herb, B – tree, C – creeper, D – shrub", "A – herb, B – shrub, C – creeper, D – tree", "A – tree, B – herb, C – shrub, D – creeper"], answer: 1, explain: "The stem tells the type: soft stem herb, woody trunk tree, weak stem along the ground creeper, many hard thin stems shrub.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A highway is planned through a forest. Which result is most likely to reduce biodiversity?", options: ["Animals lose their homes and food, and others that depend on them are affected", "More shelter becomes available for forest animals", "Animals will adapt to the new road in one day", "There will be no effect, because trees are only plants"], answer: 0, explain: "Living things depend on one another for food and shelter, so losing a habitat can affect many kinds of organisms.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which idea about adaptation is correct?", options: ["An organism changes its habitat whenever it likes", "All organisms have the same adaptations", "A feature that helps an organism survive in its habitat may not suit another habitat", "A habit learned in one week is an adaptation"], answer: 2, explain: "Adaptations suit a particular habitat. A cactus is adapted to a desert but would not survive in a pond.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name two plants that have parallel venation in their leaves.", a: "Any two: wheat, maize, grass, banana.", marks: 1, type: "short" },
      { q: "Write two differences between a herb and a tree.", a: "A herb is small and has a soft, green, tender stem (mint, coriander). A tree is tall and has a thick, hard, woody trunk with branches high above the ground (mango, neem).", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Distinguish between terrestrial and aquatic habitats. Give two examples of each.", a: "Terrestrial habitats are on land, e.g. forest, desert. Aquatic habitats are in water, e.g. pond, river, sea (any two of each).", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Why does a camel have broad padded feet? What does its hump store?", a: "Broad padded feet stop the feet from sinking in soft sand. The hump stores fat.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Write two functions of roots.", a: "Roots hold the plant firmly in the soil and absorb water and minerals from the soil.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "What are tendrils? Name two climbers.", a: "Tendrils are thread-like parts with which a weak-stemmed plant holds a support and climbs up. Examples of climbers: grapevine, pea.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Why can the loss of one kind of living thing affect many others? Give an example.", a: "Living things depend on one another for food and shelter. For example, if a plant disappears, the animals that eat it lose their food, and animals that eat those animals are also affected.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Name the type of root and the type of venation usually seen together in a mango plant and in a wheat plant.", a: "Mango: taproot and reticulate (net-like) venation. Wheat: fibrous roots and parallel venation.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Explain with examples how a cactus and a camel are adapted to live in a desert.", a: "Cactus: the thick stem stores water.\nCactus: leaves are reduced to spines, which reduces loss of water.\nCamel: long legs and broad padded feet help it walk on sand without sinking.\nCamel: the hump stores fat which gives it food when it is scarce.", marks: 4, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: In a school garden, Kiran noted three plants. Plant P is 8 m tall with a hard woody trunk and a taproot. Plant Q has a weak stem that spreads along the ground and bears large fruits. Plant R is a small plant with a soft green stem and leaves having veins running side by side.\n(i) Name the type of plant (herb, shrub, tree, climber or creeper) of P, Q and R.\n(ii) Which plant is likely to have fibrous roots? Give a reason.\n(iii) Suggest one way by which Kiran's school can help to protect the variety of plants and animals.", a: "(i) P is a tree, Q is a creeper and R is a herb.\n(ii) R is likely to have fibrous roots because plants with parallel venation usually have fibrous roots.\n(iii) Any one: planting trees, not harming habitats such as the school pond, not picking plants unnecessarily.", marks: 5, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "mindful-eating-a-path-to-a-healthy-body": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of these foods comes from animals?", options: ["Jaggery", "Honey", "Ragi", "Palak"], answer: 1, explain: "Honey is made by bees. Jaggery comes from sugarcane, ragi is a millet and palak is a leafy vegetable.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The brinjal that we eat as a vegetable is a", options: ["fruit", "root", "leaf", "flower"], answer: 0, explain: "Brinjal is the fruit of the plant. Carrot is a root, palak a leaf and cauliflower a flower.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Ragi, jowar and bajra are examples of", options: ["millets", "pulses", "oils", "fruits"], answer: 0, explain: "These are millets, which are staple foods in many parts of India.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Ghee and cooking oil are rich in", options: ["proteins", "vitamins", "fibre", "fats"], answer: 3, explain: "Ghee and oil are rich in fats, which also give energy.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which mineral is needed by the body to make blood?", options: ["Iron", "Calcium", "Iodine", "Vitamin D"], answer: 0, explain: "Iron is needed to make blood. Lack of iron causes anaemia. Vitamin D is a vitamin, not a mineral.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is a good source of calcium?", options: ["Jaggery", "Orange", "Iodised salt", "Ragi"], answer: 3, explain: "Ragi, milk, curd and paneer supply calcium. Jaggery gives iron, orange gives vitamin C and iodised salt gives iodine.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which is a safe food habit?", options: ["Eating stale food if it looks fine", "Keeping cooked food uncovered", "Washing hands with soap before eating", "Eating fruits without washing them"], answer: 2, explain: "Clean hands, washed fruits, covered food and fresh food keep us free from infections.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which food is a good source of dietary fibre?", options: ["Butter and cream", "Fresh vegetables and whole grains", "Sugar", "Cold drinks"], answer: 1, explain: "Fibre comes from plant foods such as vegetables, fruits and whole grains.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which food is rich in both proteins and calcium?", options: ["Jaggery", "Plain boiled rice", "Lemon", "Paneer"], answer: 3, explain: "Paneer is made from milk and gives proteins for growth and calcium for bones. Jaggery gives iron, rice gives carbohydrates and lemon gives vitamin C.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why is water important for the body?", options: ["It gives energy like sugar does", "It helps digestion, carries nutrients and removes wastes", "It builds bones and teeth like calcium", "It prevents scurvy"], answer: 1, explain: "Water is not a source of energy and does not build bones, but it helps in digestion, carries nutrients and removes wastes.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Vitamins and minerals are needed", options: ["in small amounts to keep the body healthy and protect it from disease", "in very large amounts for energy", "only by adults", "only when we are ill"], answer: 0, explain: "The body needs only small amounts of vitamins and minerals, but needs them regularly.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A bird eats grains as well as worms and insects. It is", options: ["a herbivore", "an omnivore", "a carnivore", "a vegetarian"], answer: 1, explain: "An animal that eats both plants and other animals is an omnivore.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Sushma mostly eats chapatis, rice, potatoes and sweets. Which nutrient is she getting most of?", options: ["Proteins", "Vitamins", "Fibre", "Carbohydrates"], answer: 3, explain: "These foods are rich in starch and sugar, which are carbohydrates. Her diet has few proteins and vitamins.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which tiffin is the best choice for a growing child?", options: ["A fruit and a handful of roasted chana", "A packet of chips and a cold drink", "Cream biscuits and sweets", "Fried snacks every day"], answer: 0, explain: "Fruit gives vitamins and fibre and chana gives proteins. Chips, cold drinks and sweets have too much sugar, salt and oil.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why should we read the ingredients and the expiry date on packed food?", options: ["To know how heavy it is", "To find out which shop sold it", "To know what it contains and whether it is safe to eat", "To check how many vitamins the date contains"], answer: 2, explain: "The label tells us what is in the food, and an expired food may be unsafe.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Mohan eats only rice, bread and sweets every day for many months. What is he most likely to get?", options: ["Too much fibre", "Excess of vitamins", "A deficiency disease, because his diet is not balanced", "Nothing, because rice gives all nutrients"], answer: 2, explain: "A diet lacking proteins, vitamins and minerals can cause deficiency diseases and malnutrition.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Amla, guava, lemon and orange are all good sources of", options: ["vitamin A", "vitamin C", "vitamin D", "iron"], answer: 1, explain: "These fruits are rich in vitamin C, which keeps gums healthy and helps fight infections. Carrot and papaya give vitamin A, and jaggery gives iron.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which disease is correctly paired with its cause?", options: ["Rickets – lack of vitamin C", "Goitre – lack of iron", "Night blindness – lack of vitamin A", "Anaemia – lack of calcium"], answer: 2, explain: "Rickets is from lack of vitamin D, goitre from lack of iodine and anaemia from lack of iron.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why is it good to chew food slowly and stop eating when you feel full?", options: ["It lets us eat more food", "It makes the food more nutritious", "It saves cooking time", "It helps digestion and lets us notice when we have eaten enough"], answer: 3, explain: "Eating slowly with attention is part of mindful eating. It prevents overeating.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A vegetarian family does not eat eggs, fish or meat. Which foods can still give them proteins?", options: ["Rice and sugar", "Dal and paneer", "Ghee and oil", "Lemon and amla"], answer: 1, explain: "Dal, paneer, milk and soya are good plant or milk sources of proteins.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A packet of biscuits lists sugar, palm oil and refined flour. If a child eats it daily instead of roti, dal and vegetables, what is the likely result?", options: ["The child gets mainly energy from sugar and fat but few proteins, vitamins and minerals", "The child gets more proteins and vitamins", "The child needs less water", "The child will not get tired"], answer: 0, explain: "Packaged junk food gives energy but little of the nutrients needed for growth and protection.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A girl has bleeding gums and looks pale. Which two nutrients is she most likely to be short of?", options: ["Vitamin C and iron", "Vitamin A and iodine", "Vitamin D and calcium", "Iodine and vitamin C"], answer: 0, explain: "Bleeding gums point to lack of vitamin C (scurvy) and a pale look to lack of iron (anaemia).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A person eats large amounts of food every day but still gets a deficiency disease. How is this possible?", options: ["Deficiency diseases occur only when we eat too little food", "Eating more food always prevents deficiency", "Only fruits can cause deficiency diseases", "The food may be large in quantity but may lack some nutrient"], answer: 3, explain: "A balanced diet needs the right kinds of nutrients, not just a large quantity of food.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which set matches nutrients to their roles correctly?", options: ["Carbohydrates – growth; proteins – quick energy; fibre – protection", "Fibre – energy; fats – digestion; water – growth", "Carbohydrates – quick energy; proteins – growth and repair; fibre – digestion", "Proteins – digestion; vitamins – energy; carbohydrates – repair"], answer: 2, explain: "Carbohydrates give quick energy, proteins help growth and repair, and fibre helps digestion.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A student tests foods with iodine solution. Boiled potato and cooked rice turn blue-black; milk stays brown. Which conclusion is correct?", options: ["Milk contains more starch than potato", "Iodine turns black on all foods", "Potato and rice contain starch; milk does not", "All three foods have starch"], answer: 2, explain: "Iodine turns blue-black only where starch is present.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name two foods that give us fibre and write one use of fibre.", a: "Fruits, vegetables and whole grains (any two). Fibre helps digestion and prevents constipation.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Write the functions of carbohydrates and of proteins, with one food source of each.", a: "Carbohydrates give quick energy (rice, wheat, potato, sugar). Proteins help the body grow and repair itself (dal, milk, paneer, eggs).", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Write the source and one use of calcium and of iron.", a: "Calcium makes bones and teeth strong; sources are milk, curd, paneer and ragi. Iron is needed to make blood; sources are green leafy vegetables, jaggery, dates and pulses.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Name the vitamin whose lack causes rickets and give two ways to get it.", a: "Vitamin D. We get it from sunlight and from milk, eggs and fish.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "What is malnutrition? Can an overweight child be malnourished? Explain.", a: "Malnutrition is poor health caused by too little, too much or the wrong balance of nutrients. Yes, an overweight child who eats too much junk food can be malnourished.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Why should we wash fruits and vegetables and keep cooked food covered?", a: "Washing removes dirt and germs, and covering cooked food keeps flies and dust away. Both help to keep food safe and prevent infections.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Write two reasons why a person should eat slowly and without a phone or TV.", a: "Eating slowly helps digestion and lets us notice when we are full. Without a screen we pay attention to the food, eat the right amount and do not waste it.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Explain what a balanced diet is. Write a lunch menu for a school child that is balanced and say which nutrient each item gives.", a: "A balanced diet has all nutrients in the right amounts for a person's age, size and activity.\nExample menu: roti or rice (carbohydrates for energy).\nDal or paneer (proteins for growth and repair).\nGreen vegetable or salad (vitamins, minerals and fibre).\nFruit or a glass of milk (vitamins and calcium).", marks: 4, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Seema's brother Ajay is 8 years old. His legs are bent, he feels very tired and he often has bleeding gums. He eats mainly rice, chips and sweets, rarely plays outdoors and drinks no milk.\n(i) Name two deficiency diseases that Ajay is likely to have.\n(ii) Which nutrient is lacking in each?\n(iii) Suggest two changes in his food habits.", a: "(i) Rickets and scurvy.\n(ii) Rickets is due to lack of vitamin D (and calcium); scurvy is due to lack of vitamin C.\n(iii) Any two: drink milk, eat citrus fruits or amla, eat dal and vegetables, play outdoors in sunlight, eat less chips and sweets.", marks: 4, type: "case" },
      { q: "Dr Kaur finds that in a village many adults have a swelling in the neck, many children look pale and tired, and the villagers eat mainly rice and plain salt. (a) Name the two deficiency diseases. (b) Name the nutrient lacking in each. (c) Suggest one change in food for each problem.", a: "(a) Goitre (swelling in the neck) and anaemia (pale and tired).\n(b) Goitre: iodine. Anaemia: iron.\n(c) Goitre: use iodised salt. Anaemia: eat green leafy vegetables, jaggery, dates and pulses.\nBoth can be prevented by a balanced, varied diet.", marks: 5, type: "hots" }
    ]
  },
  "exploring-magnets": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which pair of objects are both attracted by a magnet?", options: ["An iron pin and a steel paper clip", "A wooden ruler and a plastic comb", "A copper wire and aluminium foil", "A glass marble and a rubber band"], answer: 0, explain: "Iron and steel are magnetic. Wood, plastic, copper, aluminium, glass and rubber are not.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which shape of magnet looks like the letter U?", options: ["Bar magnet", "Ring magnet", "Cylindrical magnet", "Horseshoe magnet"], answer: 3, explain: "A horseshoe magnet is bent like a U. A bar magnet is straight and a ring magnet is a round ring.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Small soft iron pieces placed across the ends of bar magnets when they are stored are called", options: ["poles", "needles", "keepers", "compasses"], answer: 2, explain: "Keepers help magnets from losing their strength.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A magnetic compass needle is", options: ["a piece of copper", "a plastic strip", "a small magnet that can turn freely", "a piece of plain iron that is not a magnet"], answer: 2, explain: "Because the needle is a small magnet, it settles in the north–south direction.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The end of a freely hanging magnet that points towards the north is its", options: ["south pole", "north pole", "keeper", "centre"], answer: 1, explain: "The end of the magnet that points north is called the north pole.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A magnet pulls an iron nail without touching it. This shows that", options: ["a magnet can act from a distance", "a magnet must touch the object it pulls", "the nail is a magnet", "the nail is non-magnetic"], answer: 0, explain: "The pull of a magnet acts across a gap, without touching.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A magnet attracts a steel paper clip but not a plastic cap. The paper clip is made of a", options: ["non-magnetic material", "natural magnet", "lodestone", "magnetic material"], answer: 3, explain: "Materials attracted by a magnet are called magnetic materials. Plastic is non-magnetic.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which pair has one magnetic and one non-magnetic material?", options: ["Copper and aluminium", "Nickel and cobalt", "Iron and wood", "Wood and plastic"], answer: 2, explain: "Iron is magnetic and wood is not. Copper, aluminium, wood and plastic are non-magnetic; nickel and cobalt are magnetic.", level: 1 },
      { q: "An iron nail is attracted by a magnet even when a sheet of paper is kept between them. The reason is that", options: ["paper is a magnetic material", "the pull of a magnet passes through thin non-magnetic materials", "the nail is a magnet", "the paper turns into a magnet"], answer: 1, explain: "Paper is non-magnetic and thin, so the magnet's pull passes through it.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Two magnets are brought close and push each other away. This shows that", options: ["the poles facing each other are unlike", "one of them is only a piece of iron", "the poles facing each other are alike", "both are non-magnetic"], answer: 2, explain: "Only like poles repel. A piece of iron can never push a magnet away.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of these can weaken a magnet?", options: ["Storing it with a keeper", "Keeping a pair with unlike poles side by side", "Dropping it on the floor many times", "Stroking a needle with it"], answer: 2, explain: "Heating, hammering or dropping weakens a magnet. Keepers and correct storage protect it.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why should a compass not be kept near a strong magnet?", options: ["The compass becomes heavier", "The magnet pulls the needle away from the north–south direction", "The magnet melts the compass glass", "The needle changes to wood"], answer: 1, explain: "A magnet near a compass disturbs the needle, so it will show a wrong direction.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Meera faces the north end of her compass needle. The school gate is on her right. In which direction is the gate?", options: ["West", "South", "North", "East"], answer: 3, explain: "When you face north, east is on your right, west on your left and south behind you.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A fridge door seal sticks to a steel door because", options: ["both of them are non-magnetic", "the seal is made of glue", "the steel door repels the seal", "the seal contains a magnet and the steel door is a magnetic material"], answer: 3, explain: "The magnet in the seal is attracted to the steel of the fridge door.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A bar magnet is not marked. How can you find its north pole with a thread?", options: ["Break it into two pieces", "Heat it and see which end glows", "Put it near copper wire", "Hang it freely on a thread and see which end points north"], answer: 3, explain: "A freely suspended magnet comes to rest in the north–south direction. The end pointing north is its north pole.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A student tests five objects with a magnet. Iron nail – attracted. Plastic comb – not attracted. Steel needle – attracted. Wooden ruler – not attracted. Copper wire – not attracted. How many of these objects are made of magnetic material?", options: ["2", "1", "3", "5"], answer: 0, explain: "Only the iron nail and the steel needle are attracted, so only they are made of magnetic material.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of these will be repelled by the north pole of a magnet?", options: ["The north pole of another magnet", "An iron nail", "A steel pin", "The south pole of another magnet"], answer: 0, explain: "Only like poles repel. Iron and steel are only attracted. The south pole is attracted.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A boy finds a shiny coin that sticks to his magnet. What can he conclude?", options: ["The coin must itself be a magnet", "The coin has a magnetic material such as iron or nickel", "The coin is made of pure silver", "Coins can never be attracted to magnets"], answer: 1, explain: "Attraction shows only that the coin has a magnetic material. To prove that it is a magnet, we need to see repulsion.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A freely hanging bar magnet is turned by hand until it points east–west and then let go. What does it do?", options: ["It stays pointing east–west", "It swings back and settles in the north–south direction", "It falls straight down", "It keeps spinning in a circle for ever"], answer: 1, explain: "The Earth behaves like a giant magnet, so a freely turning magnet always settles in the north–south direction.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A magnetic catch holds a cupboard door shut. Which of these must be fixed on the door for it to work?", options: ["A piece of wood", "A piece of plastic", "A piece of iron", "A piece of aluminium"], answer: 2, explain: "The magnet needs a magnetic material or another magnet. Wood, plastic and aluminium are non-magnetic.", level: 2 },
      { q: "End a of a metal bar is attracted by the north pole of a magnet, and end b of the same bar is repelled by it. What can we conclude?", options: ["The bar is plain iron", "The bar is a magnet; end a is a south pole and end b is a north pole", "End a is a north pole and end b is a south pole", "The bar is a non-magnetic material"], answer: 1, explain: "Only a magnet can show repulsion. A north pole attracts a south pole and repels a north pole.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Sunil keeps his compass on a steel almirah while checking directions and the needle points in the wrong direction. Why?", options: ["The steel is a magnetic material that pulls the needle", "Steel is a non-magnetic material", "The Earth has lost its magnetism", "Compasses work only outdoors"], answer: 0, explain: "Iron objects such as steel almirahs attract the needle, so keep a compass away from them.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which statement about magnets is correct?", options: ["Repulsion can be shown only between two magnets", "Attraction between two objects proves both are magnets", "A magnet loses its poles when it is cut in the middle", "A copper wire is attracted because it is a metal"], answer: 0, explain: "Attraction also occurs between a magnet and iron. When cut, each piece has two poles. Copper is non-magnetic.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two bar magnets are kept together for months with the north pole of one next to the north pole of the other and no keepers. What is most likely to happen?", options: ["They may lose strength", "They will become stronger", "They will turn into plain iron at once", "They will become non-magnetic copper"], answer: 0, explain: "Correct storage has unlike poles side by side with keepers. Like poles side by side tend to weaken magnets.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two ring magnets are slid onto a pencil. The upper one seems to float above the lower one. What is the reason?", options: ["Unlike poles face each other and attract", "The upper one is made of plain iron", "Gravity does not act on magnets", "Like poles face each other and repel"], answer: 3, explain: "Floating shows repulsion, which happens between like poles. Attraction would make the rings stick together.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a lodestone?", a: "A lodestone is a natural magnet, found in nature.", marks: 1, type: "short" },
      { q: "Write two uses of magnets.", a: "Any two: fridge door seals, door catches, pencil boxes, magnetic toys, cranes that lift scrap iron, compasses.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Name two materials that are not attracted by a magnet and one that is.", a: "Not attracted: wood, plastic, glass, paper, copper, aluminium (any two). Attracted: iron, nickel, cobalt or steel (any one).", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "What happens when a bar magnet is cut into two pieces? Is it possible to get a single pole?", a: "Each piece becomes a smaller magnet with a north pole and a south pole. A single pole cannot be separated.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Can a magnet pull an iron pin through a sheet of paper? Explain.", a: "Yes. The pull of a magnet can pass through thin non-magnetic materials such as paper, and a magnet can act without touching the object.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Why does a freely hanging bar magnet always come to rest pointing north–south?", a: "The Earth behaves like a giant magnet, so a freely turning magnet lines up in the north–south direction. The end pointing north is its north pole.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "State the difference between a magnetic and a non-magnetic material with an example of each.", a: "A magnetic material is attracted by a magnet (iron, nickel, cobalt, steel). A non-magnetic material is not attracted (wood, plastic, glass, copper, aluminium).", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Describe how you would store a pair of bar magnets and explain why it is done this way.", a: "Place the two magnets side by side with the north pole of one next to the south pole of the other (unlike poles side by side).\nPlace soft iron pieces (keepers) across the ends.\nThis prevents the magnets from losing their strength.\nKeep them away from heat, hammering or dropping.", marks: 4, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Leela's school box has a magnetic catch. She finds that her bar magnet picks up iron nails and steel pins from a heap, but not plastic buttons or aluminium clips. She then brings the south pole of her magnet near a second bar and sees that it is pushed away.\n(i) Why are the buttons and aluminium clips not picked up?\n(ii) What does the push tell her about the second bar?\n(iii) What would she see if the second bar was just a piece of iron?", a: "(i) Plastic and aluminium are non-magnetic materials, so they are not attracted.\n(ii) It is a magnet, because only a magnet can repel; the end near her magnet is a south pole.\n(iii) The iron bar would only be attracted and never pushed away.", marks: 4, type: "case" },
      { q: "You have three metal bars that look alike. One is a magnet, one is a piece of iron and one is aluminium. You have only a bar magnet whose poles are known and no other tool. Explain how you would identify the magnet.", a: "Bring one end of each unknown bar near a pole of your bar magnet. The aluminium bar is not attracted at all, so it is non-magnetic.\nThe other two bars are attracted. Now bring both ends of each of these two bars, one after the other, near the same pole of your magnet.\nThe bar that is pushed away at one end is the magnet, because only a magnet can repel.\nThe bar that is only attracted at both ends and never repelled is the piece of iron.", marks: 5, type: "hots" }
    ]
  },
  "measurement-of-length-and-motion": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which unit is the most suitable for the thickness of a coin?", options: ["Metre", "Kilometre", "Millimetre", "Centimetre"], answer: 2, explain: "A coin is only a millimetre or two thick, so mm is the right unit. cm is better for a pencil and m for a classroom.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is the longest length?", options: ["1 m", "1500 mm", "90 cm", "950 mm"], answer: 1, explain: "Change all to mm: 1 m = 1000 mm, 90 cm = 900 mm, 950 mm, and 1500 mm is the largest.", level: 1 },
      { q: "3 cm is equal to", options: ["0.3 mm", "300 mm", "3000 mm", "30 mm"], answer: 3, explain: "1 cm = 10 mm, so 3 cm = 3 × 10 = 30 mm.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A stone tied to a string and whirled around shows", options: ["circular motion", "rectilinear motion", "rotational motion", "oscillatory motion"], answer: 0, explain: "The stone moves along a circular path.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Earth going round the Sun is an example of", options: ["rectilinear motion", "oscillatory motion", "rotational motion about its own axis", "periodic motion"], answer: 3, explain: "The Earth's path round the Sun repeats after equal intervals of time (a year), so it is periodic.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A vibrating guitar string shows", options: ["rectilinear motion", "oscillatory motion", "circular motion", "rotational motion"], answer: 1, explain: "The string moves to and fro about its fixed position.", level: 1 },
      { q: "SI stands for", options: ["International System of Units", "Standard Indian units", "Scientific Index of units", "Simple Instrument units"], answer: 0, explain: "Scientists all over the world use the International System of Units, whose unit of length is the metre.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is the most suitable to measure the length of your classroom?", options: ["A 15 cm school ruler", "A measuring tape marked in metres", "A thermometer", "A pencil"], answer: 1, explain: "A tape marked in metres lets us measure a long distance in few steps.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which conversion is correct?", options: ["250 cm = 25 m", "250 cm = 0.25 m", "250 cm = 2.5 m", "250 cm = 2500 m"], answer: 2, explain: "1 m = 100 cm, so 250 cm ÷ 100 = 2.5 m. We divide when changing to a bigger unit.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A bus route is 12.5 km long. Its length in metres is", options: ["12 500 m", "1250 m", "125 000 m", "12.5 m"], answer: 0, explain: "1 km = 1000 m, so 12.5 × 1000 = 12 500 m.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A student measures the width of a book as 21.6 and writes only that. What is missing from her answer?", options: ["The name of the book", "The date of measurement", "The unit, such as cm", "The weight of the book"], answer: 2, explain: "A number without a unit has no meaning. Always write the unit along with the number.", level: 2 },
      { q: "What is the best way to find the distance around a tree trunk?", options: ["Place a straight ruler across the trunk and read from the side", "Measure its height and double it", "Wrap a thread around the trunk, mark it, straighten the thread and measure it with a ruler", "Time how long it takes to walk past the trunk"], answer: 2, explain: "A curved length is measured with a thread, which is then straightened against a ruler.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A child in a moving bus says the roadside trees are moving backwards. Why does he think so?", options: ["He uses the bus as his reference point, and the trees change their position compared with it", "The trees really walk in the opposite direction", "Motion does not depend on any reference point", "The trees are rotating about the ground"], answer: 0, explain: "Rest and motion are relative. Compared with the bus, the trees change position; compared with the ground, they stay still.", level: 2 },
      { q: "An object is at rest if", options: ["it is very heavy", "it is placed on the ground", "it is very far from the reference point", "its position does not change with time compared with a reference point"], answer: 3, explain: "Rest means no change of position with time, compared with a reference point.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of these is rotational motion?", options: ["A car on a straight road", "A pendulum swinging", "A spinning potter's wheel", "A stone falling from a roof"], answer: 2, explain: "A potter's wheel spins about a fixed line (axis) through it.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which pair is correctly matched?", options: ["Swing – rotational; top – oscillatory", "Falling apple – circular; swing – rectilinear", "Whirled stone – rectilinear; fan – oscillatory", "Swing – oscillatory; top – rotational"], answer: 3, explain: "A swing moves to and fro, a top spins on its axis, an apple falls in a straight line and a whirled stone moves in a circle.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A 3.6 m ribbon is cut into two pieces. One piece is 150 cm long. How long is the other piece?", options: ["510 cm", "210 cm", "2.1 cm", "90 cm"], answer: 1, explain: "3.6 m = 360 cm. The other piece is 360 − 150 = 210 cm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The distance from Rani's home to school is 1.2 km and from school to the market is 800 m. How far is the market from her home if she goes via the school?", options: ["2 km", "2 m", "20 km", "0.4 km"], answer: 0, explain: "1.2 km = 1200 m. 1200 m + 800 m = 2000 m = 2 km.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Amrit's one pace is 60 cm long. How many paces will he take to cover 12 m in a straight line?", options: ["2", "20", "200", "72"], answer: 1, explain: "12 m = 1200 cm and 1200 ÷ 60 = 20.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Anu, Binu and Chitra measure the same table with their hand spans and get 8, 10 and 9 spans. What is the best explanation?", options: ["Two of them measured wrongly", "The table changes its length with the person", "A hand span is the standard unit of length", "Their spans are of different sizes, so a standard unit should be used"], answer: 3, explain: "Non-standard units differ from person to person, so results do not agree.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A ball lies still on the floor of a train moving at a steady speed. Which statement is correct?", options: ["The ball is at rest compared with the train and in motion compared with the ground outside", "The ball is at rest compared with every object", "The ball is in motion compared with the train and at rest compared with the ground", "The ball is in motion compared with every object"], answer: 0, explain: "Rest and motion depend on the reference point. The ball does not change its position on the floor, but it moves along with the train relative to the ground.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A thread laid along a curved path on a map starts at the 3.0 cm mark of the ruler and ends at the 18.0 cm mark. On the map 1 cm stands for 200 m. What is the real length of the path?", options: ["3.6 km", "15 km", "3 km", "0.3 km"], answer: 2, explain: "Thread length = 18.0 − 3.0 = 15 cm. 15 × 200 m = 3000 m = 3 km.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these motions is periodic but not oscillatory?", options: ["A swing", "A pendulum", "A vibrating guitar string", "The Earth going round the Sun"], answer: 3, explain: "The Earth repeats its path every year, so it is periodic, but it does not move to and fro about a fixed position.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The 0 to 1 cm part of a ruler is broken. A student places a pencil's end on the 1 cm mark and reads the other end as 14.5 cm. She says the pencil is 14.5 cm long. What is wrong?", options: ["Nothing is wrong, because the last reading is the length", "The reading at the start must be subtracted, so the length is 13.5 cm", "The pencil is 15.5 cm long", "The pencil is 1 cm long"], answer: 1, explain: "When an object does not start at zero, length = last reading − first reading = 14.5 − 1.0 = 13.5 cm.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Sachin sits in a stationary train. The train next to his starts moving forward slowly, and for a moment he feels that his own train is moving backwards. What is the best explanation?", options: ["He judges motion with the other moving train as his reference point, and motion is relative", "His train really moved backwards", "The platform moved backwards", "Both trains were at rest"], answer: 0, explain: "Without looking at a fixed object such as the platform, he mistakes the motion of the other train for his own.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the SI unit of length and write its symbol.", a: "The SI unit of length is the metre, symbol m.", marks: 1, type: "short" },
      { q: "Express (a) 1.8 m in millimetres and (b) 2500 mm in metres.", a: "(a) 1.8 × 1000 = 1800 mm.\n(b) 2500 ÷ 1000 = 2.5 m.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Why can we not use a hand span or a foot as a standard unit?", a: "They differ from person to person, so the measurements would not agree. A standard unit is the same for everyone.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "What is a reference point? Give an example.", a: "A reference point is a fixed point against which we decide whether an object is at rest or in motion. For example, a tree beside the road is a reference point for a moving bus.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Give one example each of circular and oscillatory motion.", a: "Circular: a stone tied to a string and whirled around. Oscillatory: a swing, a pendulum or a vibrating guitar string.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "The zero end of a ruler is worn off. How can you still measure the length of an eraser correctly? Write the steps.", a: "Start from a clear full mark, such as 1 cm. Note the reading where the eraser ends. Subtract the first reading from the last reading to get the length.", marks: 2, type: "short" },
      { q: "Radha's shop sells a ribbon of 2.4 m and a wire of 175 cm. Write both lengths in centimetres and say which is longer.", a: "2.4 m = 2.4 × 100 = 240 cm. The wire is 175 cm. The ribbon (240 cm) is longer by 65 cm.", marks: 3, type: "short" },
      { q: "Describe how you would measure the length of a curved road on a map, and calculate its real length if the thread measures 18 cm and the scale is 1 cm = 2 km.", a: "Lay a thread along the road on the map and mark its two ends on the thread.\nStraighten the thread and hold it against a ruler to read its length (18 cm).\nReal length = 18 × 2 km.\nReal length = 36 km.", marks: 4, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Gagan sits on a swing in the park while her friend stands near a tree. A bus passes along the straight road outside the park. A ceiling fan runs in the park office.\n(i) Name the type of motion of the swing.\n(ii) Name the type of motion of the bus.\n(iii) Is Gagan at rest or in motion compared with the tree? Give a reason.", a: "(i) The swing shows oscillatory (to-and-fro) motion, which is also periodic.\n(ii) The bus shows rectilinear motion (along a straight line).\n(iii) She is in motion compared with the tree because her position changes with time compared with it. Rest and motion depend on the reference point.", marks: 4, type: "case" },
      { q: "Three students measure the same curved edge of a leaf. Meena uses a thread and a ruler. Bunty uses a straight ruler laid across the leaf. Sonu uses a hand span. (a) Who will get the best measurement of the curved edge? (b) Why will the other two methods be less useful? (c) Write the unit that Meena should use for the length of the leaf.", a: "(a) Meena gets the best measurement.\n(b) A straight ruler measures a straight line, not a curved one, and a hand span is not a standard unit, so it gives different results for different persons.\n(c) She should use centimetres (cm), because the leaf is small. The unit must be written with the number.", marks: 5, type: "hots" }
    ]
  },
  "materials-around-us": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of these is obtained directly from plants?", options: ["Wool", "Silk", "Leather", "Jute"], answer: 3, explain: "Jute comes from the jute plant. Wool, silk and leather come from animals.", level: 1, obj: "object-material" },
      { q: "Glass is made by people from", options: ["petroleum", "sand", "wood", "clay"], answer: 1, explain: "Glass is made from sand. Plastic is made from petroleum and paper from wood or bamboo.", level: 1, obj: "object-material" },
      { q: "A pencil is made of wood, graphite and rubber. This shows that", options: ["one material can make only one object", "one object can be made of several materials", "a pencil is made only of natural materials", "graphite is a liquid"], answer: 1, explain: "Many objects use more than one material, and one material, such as wood, can make many objects.", level: 1, obj: "object-material" },
      { q: "Which of these is soft and easy to press?", options: ["An iron nail", "A stone", "A steel spoon", "A sponge"], answer: 3, explain: "Sponge, cotton and wax are soft. Iron, stone and steel are hard.", level: 1, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "Which of these lets you see clearly through it?", options: ["Plain water", "Butter paper", "A wooden door", "A stone slab"], answer: 0, explain: "Plain water is transparent. Butter paper is translucent, and wood and stone are opaque.", level: 1, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "Spectacle lenses are made of clear glass because glass is", options: ["transparent", "translucent", "opaque", "soluble"], answer: 0, explain: "Transparent materials let us see clearly through them, so we can see through spectacles.", level: 1, obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "Which of these is biodegradable?", options: ["A jute bag", "A plastic carry bag", "A plastic bottle", "A thermocol cup"], answer: 0, explain: "Jute is a natural material that microbes in soil can break down. Plastic and thermocol last for a very long time.", level: 1, obj: "use-wisely" },
      { q: "The 3 Rs that help us to cut down waste are", options: ["reduce, rest and repair", "repair, replace and reject", "read, reuse and rethink", "reduce, reuse and recycle"], answer: 3, explain: "Reduce the use of materials, reuse things, and recycle paper, metal and glass.", level: 1, obj: "use-wisely" },
      { q: "Rakesh drops a thermocol plate, a steel spoon, an empty plastic bottle with its cap closed and a stone into a bucket of water. How many of these sink?", options: ["1", "3", "4", "2"], answer: 3, explain: "The steel spoon and the stone sink. The thermocol plate and the closed empty bottle float.", level: 2, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "Which of these materials is translucent?", options: ["Clear glass", "A wooden door", "Frosted glass", "Air"], answer: 2, explain: "Frosted glass lets some light through but we cannot see clearly through it. Clear glass and air are transparent, and wood is opaque.", level: 2, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "How can you test whether a white powder is soluble in water?", options: ["Drop it in oil and see whether it floats", "Hold a magnet near it", "Stir a spoonful of it in a glass of water and see whether it disappears", "Press it between your fingers"], answer: 2, explain: "A soluble powder dissolves and the water looks clear. An insoluble powder stays visible or settles at the bottom.", level: 2, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "Which pair has one hard and one soft material?", options: ["Stone and iron", "Iron and wax", "Cotton and sponge", "Sponge and wax"], answer: 1, explain: "Iron is hard, wax is soft. Stone and iron are both hard; cotton, sponge and wax are all soft.", level: 2, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "Which statement about floating and sinking is correct?", options: ["A big wooden log floats, while a small iron nail sinks", "All heavy things sink and all light things float", "All metals float on water", "A bigger object always sinks"], answer: 0, explain: "Whether something floats depends on the material, not only on its size or heaviness.", level: 2, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "A company must choose a material for a school bag that does not tear easily and does not soak up rain. Which is the best choice?", options: ["Paper", "Strong waterproof cloth", "A soft sponge", "Cotton wool"], answer: 1, explain: "The job needs a strong, waterproof material. Paper and cotton wool tear or soak up water, and a sponge absorbs water.", level: 2, obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "Mother cooks dal in a steel pot. Which property of steel makes it suitable for a cooking vessel?", options: ["It floats on water", "It is hard and allows heat to pass through it", "It is translucent", "It does not let heat pass"], answer: 1, explain: "Cooking vessels are made of metals because they are hard and allow heat to pass, so the food gets hot.", level: 2, obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "A cotton cloth wipes spilt water from a table better than a plastic sheet because cotton", options: ["is transparent", "floats on water", "absorbs water", "dissolves in water"], answer: 2, explain: "Cotton is soft and absorbs water. Plastic does not absorb water.", level: 2, obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "A banana peel thrown on the soil disappears in a few weeks, but a plastic bag stays for years. Why?", options: ["Plastic is heavier than the peel", "The peel dissolves in the soil", "Soil can break down only man-made things", "Microbes in the soil can break down the peel but not easily the plastic"], answer: 3, explain: "Biodegradable materials are broken down naturally by microbes. Plastic does not rot easily.", level: 2, obj: "use-wisely" },
      { q: "Meena uses an empty glass jam jar to store spices at home. This is an example of", options: ["reduce", "recycle", "reuse", "a biodegradable material"], answer: 2, explain: "Using the same thing again, without making it into something new, is reuse. Recycling needs the material to be processed again.", level: 2, obj: "use-wisely" },
      { q: "In tea, the sugar seems to disappear, yet the tea tastes sweet. This is because the sugar", options: ["has dissolved in the tea", "has become miscible with the cup", "has floated away", "has turned into water"], answer: 0, explain: "Sugar is soluble. It dissolves, so it cannot be seen, but it is still present in the tea. Miscible describes two liquids that mix.", level: 2, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "Which list contains only materials that come straight from nature?", options: ["Cotton, glass, wood", "Plastic, clay, wool", "Cotton, clay, wood", "Clay, paper, sand"], answer: 2, explain: "Cotton, clay and wood come directly from nature. Glass, plastic and paper are made by people from natural materials.", level: 2, obj: "object-material" },
      { q: "The table shows tests on four materials. P: floats, does not let light pass. Q: sinks, dissolves in water. R: sinks, does not dissolve, lets light pass clearly. S: sinks, does not dissolve, does not let light pass. Which material is the best for making a water bottle that can be seen through?", options: ["P", "R", "Q", "S"], answer: 1, explain: "R is transparent and insoluble, so it holds water and shows what is inside. Q would dissolve in water, and P and S are opaque.", level: 3, obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "A kettle should heat water quickly, and its handle should stay cool enough to hold. Which combination of materials is best?", options: ["A steel body and a plastic handle", "A plastic body and a steel handle", "A wooden body and a steel handle", "A steel body and a steel handle"], answer: 0, explain: "Metal lets heat pass, so it heats the water quickly, but it would also make the handle hot. Plastic does not get hot easily.", level: 3, obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "Raj says, 'Glass is a natural material, because it is made from sand and sand is found on the beach.' What is the best reply?", options: ["He is right, because anything made from sand is natural", "Sand is also made by people", "Glass is made from petroleum", "Sand is natural, but glass is made by people from sand"], answer: 3, explain: "A material is natural if it comes straight from nature. Glass has to be made by people.", level: 3, obj: "object-material" },
      { q: "Which row correctly gives an example of each type of material?", options: ["Transparent – clear glass; translucent – butter paper; opaque – a wooden door", "Transparent – butter paper; translucent – clear glass; opaque – a wooden door", "Transparent – clear glass; translucent – a wooden door; opaque – butter paper", "Transparent – a wooden door; translucent – butter paper; opaque – clear glass"], answer: 0, explain: "Transparent materials show things clearly, translucent ones show them blurred, and opaque ones do not allow light through at all.", level: 3, obj: "properties" },
      { q: "A school's waste in one week: vegetable peels 12 kg, paper 8 kg, plastic 5 kg and glass 3 kg. How much of the waste is biodegradable?", options: ["28 kg", "12 kg", "20 kg", "8 kg"], answer: 2, explain: "Vegetable peels and paper are broken down by microbes: 12 + 8 = 20 kg. Plastic and glass stay for very long.", level: 3, obj: "use-wisely" }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name two materials made by people and the natural material from which each is made.", a: "Any two: glass from sand; paper from wood or bamboo; plastic from petroleum.", marks: 2, type: "short", obj: "object-material" },
      { q: "How is an object different from a material? Show with one example that an object can be made of more than one material.", a: "An object is a thing we use, such as a tumbler or a chair; a material is what it is made of, such as glass or wood. A pencil is one object made of wood, graphite and rubber.", marks: 2, type: "short", obj: "object-material" },
      { q: "What is lustre? Name two materials that have it.", a: "Lustre is the shine of the surface of a material. Metals such as gold, silver and copper have lustre.", marks: 1, type: "short", obj: "properties" },
      { q: "Write two differences between miscible and immiscible liquids with one example of each.", a: "Miscible liquids mix completely and form one clear liquid (water and vinegar). Immiscible liquids do not mix and form separate layers (oil and water).", marks: 2, type: "short", obj: "properties" },
      { q: "Why is a cotton towel used to dry our body, and a plastic sheet used to cover a vegetable cart during rain?", a: "Cotton is soft and absorbs water, so it dries us. Plastic sheet is waterproof, so it keeps the vegetables dry.", marks: 2, type: "short", obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "Which material would you choose for (a) the knob on the lid of a hot pressure cooker and (b) a cover for a school bag in the monsoon? Give a reason for each.", a: "(a) Plastic or wood, because it does not heat up easily, so the knob can be held safely. (b) A waterproof material such as plastic or coated cloth, because it does not let rainwater pass.", marks: 2, type: "short", obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "What does biodegradable mean? Give two examples of biodegradable materials.", a: "Biodegradable means that microbes in the soil can break the material down naturally. Examples: paper, vegetable peels, cotton, jute.", marks: 2, type: "short", obj: "use-wisely" },
      { q: "Case study: Imran put five things in water, one at a time: a thermocol ball, a pinch of salt, a pinch of chalk powder, a few drops of vinegar and a few drops of cooking oil. He stirred each one.\n(i) Which of these dissolve in water?\n(ii) Which forms a separate layer?\n(iii) Which one floats when dropped in water as a solid?", a: "(i) Salt dissolves in water, and vinegar mixes completely with water (miscible); chalk powder does not dissolve.\n(ii) Cooking oil forms a separate layer, because oil and water are immiscible.\n(iii) The thermocol ball floats on water.", marks: 4, type: "case", obj: "properties" },
      { q: "A school plans a new rainy-day waiting shed. The roof must keep rain out and let daylight in. The seat must not become too hot in the sun and should not absorb water. (a) Choose a suitable material for the roof and give two properties that make it suitable. (b) Choose a suitable material for the seat and give two reasons.", a: "(a) A waterproof, transparent or translucent plastic sheet (or glass). It keeps out rain because it is waterproof and lets daylight in because it lets light pass.\n(b) Wood or plastic. It does not heat up easily like metal, and it does not absorb water.\nThe material is chosen according to the property needed for the job.", marks: 5, type: "hots", obj: "choose-material" },
      { q: "Explain the 3 Rs with one example each, and say why plastic bags in the monsoon are a problem.", a: "Reduce: use fewer materials, e.g. buy only what is needed or carry a cloth or jute bag.\nReuse: use things again, e.g. an empty jar for storing spices.\nRecycle: make old paper, metal or glass into new things.\nPlastic does not rot, so plastic bags in drains block the flow of rainwater and stay in land and rivers for a very long time.", marks: 4, type: "long", obj: "use-wisely" }
    ]
  }
});
